# MOF/PCP-based Electrocatalysts for the Oxygen Reduction Reaction

Liang Tang1,2 · Qinshang $\mathsf{X}\mathsf{u}^{1,2}$ · Yu Zhang1,2 · Wenqian Chen1,2   · Minghong Wu1,2
Abstract
The oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) is the fundamental reaction at the cathode of a fuel cell. Although the commercial precious metal catalyst $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ has unique catalytic activity, its high cost, low storage capacity and poor stability limit the commercial application of fuel cells. Therefore, it is essential to explore catalysts with abundant functional materials and develop fuel cells with high activity and high stability. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers (PCPs) are highly designable structures composed of organic ligands and metal ions. Because of their large specific surface area, high porosity and tunable chemical structure, MOFs/PCPs are considered the most promising catalytic material for the ORR. This review discusses the research progress and latest development of MOF/PCP applications as ORR catalysts, including the basic principles and the design rules of MOFs/PCPs as ORR catalysts. In addition, this work also elaborates on the active sites of ORR catalysts, which originate from the MOFs/PCPs. Ultimately, we present a research review of the last 5 years and the prospects in the field of using MOFs/PCPs for the fabrication of ORR catalysts.
Keywords  Electrocatalysis $\cdot\cdot$ Oxygen reduction reaction $\cdot$ Metal–organic framework $\cdot\cdot$ Active sites $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ Dimension
## 1  Introduction
With the rapid consumption of traditional fossil fuels and the increasing severity of environmental contamination, searching for a clean, efficient and sustainable system for energy storage and conversion is crucial. It is well known that the electrochemical oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) is the basis for various types of energy storage and conversion applications and plays an irreplaceable role. Fuel batteries are one of the most promising devices for energy conversion with clean emissions and efficient transformation ability. Furthermore, the ORR plays an essential role in the process of proton exchange membrane fuel cells because it directly influences the current density, which is a crucial part of fuel battery performance [1, 2]. However, due to the slow kinetics of the ORR, Pt is commonly used as a catalyst to boost the kinetics of the ORR [3]. Due to the high cost and CO poisoning of precious metals, searching for nonprecious metals and their compounds that can be used as electrocatalysts is important. Additionally, designing a catalyst with more active sites for the acceleration of electron transfer to enhance the catalysis of the ORR is also essential [4]. In this review, we mainly focus on exploring catalysts that are inexpensive and effective for decreasing the activation energy barrier and increasing electron transfer during the ORR.
Metal–organic frameworks/porous coordination polymers (MOFs/PCPs) are polymeric coordination frameworks with regular micropore channels [5, 6]. To date, more than 82000 types of MOFs/PCPs have been reported [7]. Due to their high surface area and high designability, MOF/PCP materials can be used in various applications, including gas sorption/separation, catalysis and conductivity [8, 9]. There are various synthesis methods for MOFs/PCPs, including microwave synthesis, solvent diffusion and hydrothermal synthesis. An increasing number of MOF/PCP composites and their derivatives have been applied to electrocatalysts in recent years. In terms of their excellent designable structure and interfacial electronic coupling, MOFs/PCPs could potentially be used in the field of water splitting [10]. MOF/ PCP-based electrocatalysts can be categorized as follows:
zero-dimensional 0D, one-dimensional (1D) nanorods and nanotubes, two-dimensional (2D) nanosheets and threedimensional (3D) structures. MOF/PCP materials can be grown in different ways, such as separation growth, template growth, self-shaping growth and sacrificial agent-assisted growth [11].
In 1964, Jasinski et al. was the first to find that cobalt phthalocyanine (CoPc) possessed redox activity under alkaline conditions [12], which set a precedent for nonprecious metal catalysts. Afterward, Gupta et al. were the first to find that a metal-nitrogen-carbon (M–N-C)-structured catalyst could be synthesized without the addition of a macrocyclic compound [13]. In 2008, Xu et al.[14] was the first to use MOFs/PCPs as a self-sacrificing porous carbon template and fabricated nanoporous carbon to serve as the electrode material for an electrochemical double-layered capacitor (EDLC); this material exhibited excellent electrochemical performance. In 2009, Gong et al. were the first to report that vertically aligned nitrogen-containing carbon nanotubes (VA-NANTs) could be used as a nonmetal electrode. These VA-NANTS had better electrocatalytic performance, longterm stability and endurance than Pt under alkaline conditions [15].
It is widely thought that MOFs/PCPs are not suitable for electrochemical reactions due to their low conductivity, which to some extent inhibits charge transfer [16]. Fortunately, due to containing carbon atoms and metal atoms, the conductivities of MOF/PCP-derived materials are likely to increase by heating and turning some of the atoms into conductive atoms [17–19]. To date, MOFs/PCPs have become a common precursor for electrocatalytic synthesis and energy storage [20]. There are many reviews about MOF/PCP-based electrocatalysts, including the electrocatalytic application of carbon-based MOF/PCP catalysts, the electrocatalytic application of single-atom catalysts and the application of MOFs/PCPs for energy storage [4, 10, 17, 20–26]. However, reports on the metal and/or nonmetal active sites on MOF/ PCP-based electrocatalysts for the ORR are lacking.
The purpose of this review is to provide a comprehensive overview of MOFs/PCPs for ORR applications (Scheme 1). We mainly focus on the ORR application of MOF/PCPderived materials and introduce the basic principles and design rules for ORR catalysts, including precursor candidates for MOFs/PCPs (ZIF-8, ZIF-67, MIL-101, MOF-74, etc.) and strategies for selecting the morphology and chemical composition. Afterward, a variety of methods for synthesizing varying morphology of MOF/PCP-derived ORR catalysts are introduced in detail. In addition, we discuss the function of nonmetal active sites (C, N, S, P, etc.) and metal active sites (Fe, Co, Cu, etc.) in MOF/PCP-based ORR catalysts. We also propose the future design and prospects of MOFs/PCPs as ORR catalysts.
## 2  Design of MOFs/PCPs for catalyzing the ORR
## 2.1  ORR mechanism
The ORR is the cathodic reaction for the proton electron membrane (PEM) in a fuel battery. ORR measurements are usually conducted in an oxygen-saturated electrolyte. The onset potential and half-wave potential $(E_{1/2})$ are generally deduced by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), and these parameters are crucial indices for electrocatalytic ORR performance. There are two pathways (Fig. 1) for the ORR: the two-electron pathway and the four-electron pathway [27, 28].
The two-electron ORR pathway is mainly used for reducing hydrogen peroxide into oxygen. The equation is as follows:
Alkaline conditions:
$$
\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{HO}_{2}^{-}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}
$$
$$
\mathrm{HO_{2}}^{-}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow3\mathrm{OH}^{-}
$$
Acidic conditions:
$$
\mathrm{O}_{2}+2\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}
$$
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+2\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
The four-electron ORR pathway is mainly used for reducing oxygen into water, and the reaction is generally conducted in fuel batteries and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air batteries. The equation is as follows:
Alkaline conditions:
$$
\mathrm{O}_{2}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow4\mathrm{OH}^{-}
$$
Acidic conditions:
$$
\mathrm{O}_{2}+4\mathrm{H}^{-}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
## 2.2  ORR Performance
It is crucial to carry out a series of electrochemical characterizations to investigate the performance of ORR catalysts. Electrochemical analysis research has advanced with the development of evaluation methods. The first widely used method was electrochemical thermodynamic property measurements based on the Nernst equation and Faraday’s law of thermodynamics. Afterward, the measurement of kinetic properties was developed by controlling the electrode potential and polarization current, allowing the further study of the electrode reaction mechanism and the determination of the kinetic parameters of the reaction process [30, 31]. Combining with spectral analysis, scanning probe microscopy technology and the introduction of other parameters, such as optical signals, can significantly improve the measurement of the electrochemical properties of a system. Thus, researchers have explored various practical methods to align with the basic principle and applicable need for electrochemical tests [32, 33].
Cyclic voltammetry (CV) is one of the most commonly used methods for electrochemical research. When the electrode potential is cyclically scanned within a set potential interval at a constantly changing rate over time, the trend of changes in scan voltage is an isosceles triangle, and the current changes with the variation in electrode potential. If the first half of the scan is a cathodic process, where the active components are reduced on the electrode surface, then the second half of the scan is an anodic process, where the reduction product is reoxidized. Since the current is proportional to the electrode reaction rate during the cyclic voltammetry test, the cyclic voltammetry curve can be used to ascertain the peak potential and peak current of the reaction as well as the location and strength of the reduction peak. Additionally, it can be used to infer the reaction mechanism and calculate the kinetic parameters to further analyze the oxygen reduction activity of a material.
An LSV curve is a current curve that varies with voltage at different speeds under the condition of a continuous oxygen supply. As displayed in Fig. 2, the polarization curve is divided into three sections from right to left: the power control zone, the hybrid power zone and the diffusion control zone. The oxygen reduction activity could be evaluated via $E_{\mathrm{onset}}$ , $E_{1/2}$ and $j_{\mathrm{L}}$ .
A concurrent curve strategy is an important method to examine the stability of ORR catalysts, which is an essential factor for the commercialization of fuel cells. This method lies in measuring the variation in electrode potential while controlling the current of the working electrode. During the experiment, the oxidation current or the reduction rate applied to the electrode results in the corresponding oxidation and reduction reaction. Moreover, the electroactive material participates in the reaction at a constant rate. With the variation in the redox species concentration on the electrode surface, the electrode potential varies over time. Furthermore, the current density curve $\left(i\mathrm{-}t\right)$ , which varies over time at a specific voltage, can be measured; the slower the current density decays over time, the better the stability is [34].
Accelerated stability testing (ADT) is another essential method to test the stability of catalysts. The result can be obtained via the curve from repeated CV scanning, providing the variations in $E_{\mathrm{onset}}$ , $E_{1/2}$ and $j_{\mathrm{L}}$ . Electrochemical surface area (ECSA) analysis can be conducted to estimate the active area of the catalyst. The ECSA can be determined by hydrogen adsorption/desorption and CO stripping methods. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is a method to measure the frequency domain. With a small amplitude alternating current (AC) signal to disturb the electrode, perturbation of the system in the steady state can be obtained, which is conducive to the study of the electrode system. The EIS test has become a significant characterization method to research the process dynamics and interfacial phenomena of an electrode. Based on various EIS models, the equivalent circuit can be ascertained so that the corresponding $R_{\Omega}$ and $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ can be simulated via a computer program [35, 36].
The three-electrode system utilized for the ORR, hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) is almost identical, except for the working electrode, which is a rotating disk electrode (RDE) for the ORR (Fig. 3). An RDE can improve the uneven current on a plane electrode and the slow mass transfer speed in aqueous solution. The steady-state polarization curve at various rotation rates can be measured at a forced convection state. After the current is fully controlled by mass transfer, the theoretical limiting current density $({i_{\mathrm{d}}})$ and rotation rate match the following equation:
$$
i_{\mathrm{d}}=0.62n F A D_{0}^{2/3}\omega^{1/2}\nu^{-1/6}C_{0}
$$
$n$ : the number of transferred electrons.
A: the geometric area of the disk electrode.   
$D_{0}$ : the diffusion coefficient of the measured reactant.   
$\omega$ : the rotating angular velocity.   
$\nu$ : the solution viscosity coefficient.   
$C_{0}$ : the concentration of the analyte.
Under the actual measurement, the limited diffusion current consists of the kinetic control current $i_{\mathrm{k}}$ and the theoretical limiting current $i_{\mathrm{d}}^{\mathrm{~~}}$ which matches the Koutecky–Levich equation[37] (K–L equation):
$$
\frac{1}{i}=\frac{1}{i_{\mathrm{k}}}+\frac{1}{i_{\mathrm{d}}}=\frac{1}{i_{\mathrm{k}}}+\frac{1}{0.62n F A D_{0}^{2/3}\omega^{1/2}\nu^{-1/6}C_{0}}
$$
The number of transferred electrons $(n)$ can be obtained according to the K–L equation.
## 2.3  Design Principles of ORR Catalysts
There are many traits for ideal oxygen reduction catalysts: (1) ORR activity and stability equal to Pt metal; (2) capability of catalyzing the four-electron pathway reactions; (3) resistance to electrolytes and redox corrosion; (4) high conductivity; (5) excellent catalytic selectivity while remaining resistant to being poisoned by impurities; and (6) a high specific surface area. A stable structure and a fast proton transfer rate are also desirable. An excellent ORR catalyst serves not only to reduce the reaction barrier to break the O–O bond between oxygen molecules but also to facilitate the electron transfer and mass transfer of reactants and reaction products [38]. For the synthesis of MOFs/PCPs and their composites, it is necessary to produce a structure that is inherently conductive and electrochemically active as well as providing binding sites for oxygen generation within the porous structure [25]. Moreover, the pore sizes of $\mathrm{MOFs}/\$ PCPs are important for ORR catalysis. A large specific surface area is essential to expose active sites, while mesopores can enhance mass transfer during ORR catalysis [39, 40].
## 2.4  Representative Precursor of MOFs/PCPs for the ORR
CPs have been widely used as the precursors of ORR catalysts because of their high specific surface area and dispersed transition metal active sites. Among various kinds of MOFs/PCPs, it is necessary to select MOFs/PCPs with high stability, a simple preparation method and a high dispersion of metal active sites. To date, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) have been elected as the most commonly used precursors among all MOFs/PCPs.[11] MIL-101 has been increasingly studied due to its highly ordered porosity. Recently, Deng et al.[41] used MIL-101 and its related MOFs/PCPs as the molecular chamber to fabricate $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The obtained material not only presents high photocatalysis of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ but also lays the foundation for fabricating other materials with MIL-101. MOF-74 is a MOF/PCP with a metal center that has a low boiling point, and it is considered a commonly used precursor due to its high stability and simple synthesis process.
## 2.4.1  ZIF‑8 Precursor for the ORR
The structure of ZIFs is quite similar to that of aluminosilicate zeolites [42]. The organic linker of ZIFs is a derivative of imidazolate, which possesses a zeolite-like topological structure. A ZIF can maintain its structure at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ while becoming hollow [43].
The structure of ZIF-8 (Fig. 4a) consists of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(2\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{i}\mathbf{m})_{2}$ ( $2{\cdot}\mathbf{Mim}{=}2$ -methylimidazolate) tetrahedra, which is formed by the coordination between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ cations and 2-methylimidazolate anions; the deprotonated Mim units form bridges between the metal centers. Additionally, ZIF-8 is a material with a zeolite sodalite (SOD) topology. ZIF-8 has been increasingly studied by researchers because of its high content of carbon. ZIF-8 can be fabricated as a porous carbon material carrier via carbonization or acidification [44]. The pores of ZIF-8 are between $0.34~\mathrm{nm}$ and $1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and the thermal decomposition temperature is higher than $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [45]. Researchers usually synthesize a type of bimetallic MOFs/PCPs containing low boiling point metals (such as $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Cd) as one of the metal ions. Then, the low boiling point metals (such as $Z_{\mathrm{n-907}}\mathrm{^\circC}$ and $\mathrm{Cd-}767\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ are eliminated via high-temperature carbonization. Thus, a porous structure with excellent activity is synthesized. In situ reduction and evaporation have effectively resolved the agglomeration issue during the process of carbonization. Therefore, layered holes can be formed without the addition of an extra template [46]. Furthermore, the high porosity caused by the evaporation of the low boiling point metals can provide a large number of defects and unsaturated edge sites, which are also considered active sites that enhance ORR activity. According to Xue et al., the abundant pyridinic nitrogen (Fig. 4b and c) in ZIF-8 could be observed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, meeting the precondition for synthesizing a highly efficient ORR catalyst (Fig. 4d and e).
In addition, the carbon graphitization of ZIF-8 is beneficial to electron conductivity and corrosion resistance during the electrocatalytic process [47]. Ma et al. were the first to prove that ZIF had the capability to convert into a ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ porous carbon with ORR activity [48]. In the same year, Proietti et al. was the first to obtain a Fe–N-C catalyst in an argon and ammonia atmosphere using ZIF-8 as the precursor and loading with ferrous acetate and phenanthroline [49]. Chen et al. developed a 3D Fe–N-C structure with a template-free melamine-assisted cocalcined method (Fig. 5a), which not only dispersed highly active $\mathrm{Fe-N}_{x}$ sites but also interconnected them with mesopores. It is surprising that the performance of this ORR catalyst surpassed its commercial counterpart (Fig. 5b and c) [50]. Apart from using ZIF-8 as a precursor to synthesize some 3D particles, we can also form a hydrogen bond with the combination of 2-methylimidazole and other atoms so that a 2D structure is likely to be fabricated. Li et al.[51] reported a method that synthesized dual-metal $(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn})$ ZIF nanoleaves in an aqueous solution. With the help of a water molecule (Fig. 5d), the nitrogen atom in 2-methylimidazole forms a hydrogen bond with the hydrogen atom in the water molecule rather than coordinates with the transition metal, resulting in the formation of sodalite layers linked by the generation of ZIF nanoleaves (Fig. 5e and f). After the carbonization of dual-metal $(\mathrm{Co}/$ Zn) ZIF nanoleaves in an inert atmosphere at high temperature, the obtained carbon nanoleaves with embedded Co and doped N manifest a 2D leaf structure and high electrocatalytic ORR activity in alkaline electrolytes. After obtaining dual-metal (Ni and Co) MOF/PCP nanoleaves, Xu et al.[52] added 2-methylimidazole, which was able to coordinate with the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions on the surface of the dual-metal MOF/PCP to fabricate a 2D structure (Fig. 5g).
## 2.4.2  ZIF‑67 Precursor for the ORR
Co is usually used as the metal ion in catalysts. Additionally, with a high content of nitrogen, ZIF-67 $[\mathrm{Co}(2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Mim})_{2}]$ (Fig. 6a) is regarded as a thermally and chemically stable MOF. Owing to the merits of high yield, high purity and high chemical stability, ZIF-67 has been used as a precursor to obtain a large surface area and heteroatom-doped porous carbon material via calcination (Fig. 6b) [53]. After hightemperature decomposition, ZIF-67 is carbonized, forming $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with a large specific surface area and excellent catalytic activity. The pores of ZIF-67 are between $0.34\mathrm{nm}$ and $1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , with the thermal decomposition temperature of ZIF-67 higher than $400\ {}^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}.$ [54] Li et al. successfully synthesized a P/Ni/Co–N-C electrocatalyst (Fig. 6c and d) with polyhedral morphology and porous structure by using ZIF-67 as the precursor. The onset potential of the above electrocatalyst for the ORR reaction was $0.872{\mathrm{~V}}_{;}$ which is higher than that of Co–N-C (0.847 V) and ${\bf N i}/{\bf C o-N-C}$ (0.856 V).[55] Since the derivatives from ZIF-8/ZIF-67 tend to agglomerate during the carbonization process, the diffusion of electrolytes into the catalyst is slowed [56]. Thus, MOFs/PCPs can be encapsulated by glucosamine (Fig. 6e) to protect the MOF/PCP particles from agglomerating during pyrolysis. The nitrogen source and active metal atoms (Fe, Co, Ni, etc.) from glucosamine can provide active sites for the ORR [57]. Moreover, ZIF-67 can be used not only as the cobalt precursor but also as the morphological template. Guo et al.[58] built a bifunctional catalytic system by a twostep hydrothermal process. ZIF-67 was first transformed to hollow cobalt sulfide polyhedrons by sulfidation, and then $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets were further grown and deposited on its surface with increasing temperature.
## 2.4.3  MIL‑101 Precursor for the ORR
The typical ZIF precursor is a polyhedral structure, which means that carbon-based electrocatalysts derived from the ZIF have a few drawbacks, such as mass transfer resistance inside the pores and long diffusion lengths, resulting in a decreased active surface area and the covering of active sites [51]. MIL-101, composed of a chromium oxide cluster and terephthalate ligands, has two types of large cages with inner diameter sizes of 2.9 and $3.4~\mathrm{nm}$ and exhibits an extremely large specific surface area (Langmuir surface area $\approx5900\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ [59]. Additionally, MIL-101(Cr) $(\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{F}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{O}[(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C})-\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})]_{3}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ (Fig. 7a–e) and MIL-101(Fe) $(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O(O O C-C_{6}H_{4}-C O O)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{3}C l\cdot(H_{2}O)_{\it x}})$
have been increasingly studied owing to their large aperture and acid and alkali resistance. The synthesis procedure for these materials is quite similar, with terephthalic acid as the ligand and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{CrCl}_{3}$ as the metal source. The resulting structure exhibits an aperture of $2.9\mathrm{-}3.4~\mathrm{nm}$ [60]. Yin et al.[61] adopted a hydrothermal method to obtain an $\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{MnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ composite catalyst (Fig. 7f and g), in which $\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{{\alpha\alpha}}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}{\alpha\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}\alpha\mathbf{\alpha}\alpha\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha\alpha\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha\alpha}\alpha\alpha\mathbf\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\alpha\