## 2.4.4  MOF‑74 Precursor for the ORR
M-MOF-74 (Fig. 8a) (M/DOBDC) $\mathbf{\mathrm{M}}=\mathbf{\mathrm{Z}}\mathbf{\mathrm{n}}$ , Co, Ni, Mn, $\mathbf{Mg}$ , Fe; $\mathrm{DOBDC}=2{,}5$ -dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) is particularly interesting because of the presence of its onedimensional pores, which have a pore diameter as large as $8.5\ \mathrm{nm}\times9.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and open metal sites that result in selective gas separation [65, 66]. It is relatively stable at room temperature, with a thermal decomposition temperature that is higher than $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Furthermore, the metal center is easily replaced to form an isostructure. Abdelkader et al.[67] synthesized a noble-metal-free N,S-doped MOF-74-derived carbon-based material via a three-step method (Fig. 8b).
This material was synthesized from MOF-74 at room temperature and then underwent a low-temperature carbonization/doping treatment with the addition of thiourea at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The obtained catalyst possessed excellent ORR and OER activity. Nevertheless, the cost of the organic ligand 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid (DHTA) is too high, which is not sustainable for the synthesis of MOF-74. Hence, it is possible to replace DHTA with DHBA to synthesize a MOF-74-like material to serve as the precursor for the ORR catalyst. Dong et al.[68] improved the synthesis method for MOF-74 by replacing it with DNBA (2,5-dihydroxybenzoic acid), obtaining the MOF-74-like core–shell catalyst FeMnN-CNT (Fig. 8c and d). The half-wave of this catalyst was $0.865\mathrm{V},$ with the dynamic current density (at $0.9\mathrm{V}$ ) reaching $1.447~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , both of which are higher than those of its commercial counterpart $(0.855\mathrm{~V~}$ and $0.946~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , respectively).
## 2.5  Synthesis of MOF/PCP‑Based Catalysts for the ORR
Among all the developed catalysts, MOFs/PCPs have been widely studied due to their large specific surface area, tailorable space structure and high porosity. A large specific surface area is considered a key structural characteristic for carbon-based ORR catalysts, allowing easier access to active sites [70]. Providing active sites for the ORR reaction can be achieved by designing a series of crystalline materials with the same metal ion and an organic ligand. In addition to direct utilization, MOFs/PCPs are also used as metal oxides, functional metals, sacrificial materials and semiconductors [22]. With regard to structural integrity, MOFs/PCPs play an important role for the ORR [71]. Among all MOF/PCP composites, the combination of different types of functional materials can not only provide integral versatility but also generate new physical and chemical properties, which are not available in the individual materials [22]. There is an assortment of MOF/PCP derivatives, such as chalcogenides, oxides, hydroxides, phosphides and carbonaceous compounds, which demonstrate distinct properties [72].
To boost the catalytic ORR activity and electron transfer performance of MOF/PCP catalysts, a large number of single/dual heteroatom-doped carbon-based electrocatalysts have been synthesized. There are three different ways to fabricate MOF/PCP-based ORR catalysts. The first way is to use dipping and hydrothermal methods to dope heteroatoms after obtaining pristine MOFs/PCPs (Fig. 9a) followed by carbonizing the material to obtain a catalyst with more active sites. The second way is immediately carbonizing the pristine MOFs/PCPs after the synthesis procedure, and then, a catalyst with more active sites is obtained after heteroatom doping (Fig. 9b). The third way is obtaining MOFs/PCPs with more active sites after adding different metal salts and ligands followed by carbonizing at high temperature to obtain ORR catalysts (Fig. 9c).
## 2.6  Doping Nonmetal Atoms in MOF/PCP‑Based Catalysts for the ORR
The amount of graphitic carbon in carbon-based ORR catalysts can increase ORR activity by increasing the conductivity and the electron transfer rate [73]. There are two types of single/dual heteroatom-doped carbon-based electrocatalysts. One is a porous carbon material without nonprecious metal dopants (such as N, S, B, F, P and Se) and a porous carbon material with precious metal doping (such as Fe, Ni, Co and $\mathrm{Cu}$ ), which can further induce electron adjustment and provide an ideal electron structure [74]. The abovementioned nonprecious metal dopant atoms and precious metal dopant atoms are conducive to exposing more active sites and enhancing electrochemical performance [75]. In addition, due to the large difference in electronegativity between carbon and the dopant atoms, the catalytic performance of the material is likely to have an inhomogeneous distribution of charge [76]. The electron interaction between the dopant atoms and carbon can potentially generate a partial positive charge on the surface of these materials [77], which is helpful for the absorption and reduction of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ on carbon-based materials [74]. Therefore, the presence of dopant atoms among carbon atoms can largely increase the ORR activity [78].
In addition, the presence of a conductive support (doping with nitrogen or sulfur) promotes charge transfer on the surface of the electrolyte and prevents the aggregation of catalytic particles, which is detrimental to the durability of the material [72]. There are two kinds of methods to dope heteroatoms into coordination-based carbon materials. One way is to select MOFs/PCPs that already contain the desired dopant atom. The other way is to add a raw material into MOFs/PCPs before or during the carbonization process [79]. It is generally believed that nitrogendoped carbon materials synthesized in an inert atmosphere are likely to considerably enhance electron transfer [80]. Thus, researchers have developed metal-free carbon catalysts with myriads of dopant atoms, such as graphene, carbon nanotubes, graphite and carbon nanoshells [73]. The addition of heteroatom dopants or metal dopants can adjust the electron structure of the catalyst by combining charge effects, ligand effects and spin density, ultimately generating an overpotential [1].
Researchers have developed carbon particles doped with nitrogen as active sites by directly pyrolyzing presynthesized MOFs/PCPs. Nevertheless, the size of the bulk structure of the derived products is quite large, and the morphology is highly dependent upon the MOF/PCP template, which results in low substance transfer kinetics and a limited number of active sites. To solve this problem, a controlled low-temperature pyrolysis and selection of suitable precursors with high nitrogen content are currently the most effective methods [81]. Meng et al.[82] developed a convenient and scalable template pathway to construct hollow carbon structures with a high concentration of doped nitrogen (Fig. 10) and revealed the influence of different nitrogen species on ORR activity based on experimental analysis and theoretical calculations. They also clearly demonstrated the synthesis mechanism, including the low-pressure steam superassembly of a thin zeolite imidazolate framework (ZIF-8), the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ template to produce a shell, in situ carbonization and template removal.
## 2.7  Structural Stability of MOF/PCP‑Based Catalysts for the ORR
When MOFs/PCPs are pyrolyzed, the structure is likely to collapse, particularly in the presence of magnetic metal ions. It is generally believed that the direct pyrolysis of MOFs/PCPs cause the agglomeration of metal clusters [83]. Therefore, it is necessary to design porous carbon materials derived from MOFs/PCPs with relatively porous structures and a high degree of graphitization. Jing et al.[84] presented a convenient three-way strategy (corrosion, pyrolysis and a substitution reaction) (Fig. 11a) for fabricating cobalt, iron and nickel-codoped and nitrogenrich hollow carbon (Co/Fe/Ni–N-C) with polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP)/ZIF-8. During the synthesis process, PVP was used as the hard template to maintain the porous structure of the electrocatalyst. The research group also used $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ to etch PVP-ZIF-8-ZIF-67, which was then annealed in an argon atmosphere during aqueous treatment. The obtained $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions were replaced by $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ . The ultimate product $\mathrm{Co/Fe/Ni–N–C}$ displayed excellent catalytic ORR performance because of its high graphitization degree and abundant active sites. Wen et  al.[85] fabricated a core–shell nanocomposite material, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{C}$ , embedded with N and doped with carbon, using a brand-new 2D
Co-based MOF/PCP as the precursor and $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . Because of the protection from $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , the nitrogen content increased. The agglomeration problem during the carbonization process could be solved with the utilization of redox and the evaporation of $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , which meant there was no need for an extra template to generate layered holes. Niu et al.[46] used electrospinning to load large $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}/\mathrm{{Co}}}$ -ZIF nanocrystals with different contents on electrospun PAN nanofibers (Fig. 11b–d). After carbonization at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the obtained $\mathrm{Zn/Co-N@PCNF-800}$ material possessed good flexibility, a highly porous structure and excellent catalytic ORR performance. This convenient method not only generates layered porous structures but also enables a good distribution of metal active sites on the porous carbon nanofibers. An et al.[86] reported a free-template method without the assistance of S (Fig. 12), which could result in $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ with a high density of porous alveoli via further pyrolysis. This new strategy could increase the number of ultrasmall clusters and atomically dispersed (USCAD) Fe sites, facilitate the dispersion of Fe species, produce porous alveolar structures and emit $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ vapor, thereby eliminating the trivial template-free process. Apart from the strict control of the original structure, the coordination number of coordinated atoms and the type of coordination spheres in single-atom catalysts are also critical for ORR performance [87].
## 2.8  Stability of MOF/PCP‑Based Catalysts for the ORR in Acidic Medium
In addition to the activity of the oxygen reduction catalyst, the stability of these catalysts should be discussed. The catalytic performance of most catalysts in alkaline media is better than that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts; however, the catalytic activity and stability of these catalysts in acidic solutions still need to be further improved [88–90].
There are three reasons for the instability of a catalyst in acidic media: (1) protonation of the active site and its adjacent basic N [91]; (2) the metal dissolves chemically or electrochemically in acidic media, which leads to a decrease in the number of metal active sites, while the degradation of Nafion in the proton exchange membrane and catalytic layers causes a decrease in ionic conductivity [92–94]; (3) carbon is oxidized to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by increasing temperature or potential [95]; and (4) chemical oxidation caused by reactive oxygen species (ROS), which are produced by ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ or the Fenton reaction, which results in a decrease in the turnover frequency (TOF). The decrease in the TOF of the active sites is ascribed to the chemical oxidation caused by the synthesis of ROS via ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ or the Fenton reaction [96].
The methods to improve the stability of MOF/PCP-based catalysts for the ORR in acidic media are as follows: (1) The yield of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ can be decreased by adjusting the electronic structure of the active site. (2) A single-atom catalyst can be prepared. On one hand, the aggregation of metals can be reduced to prevent being dissolved, thereby avoiding the production of ROS and free radicals and causing chemical oxidation. On the other hand, the decrease in metal agglomeration can increase the number of $\mathbf{M}{-}\mathbf{N}_{x}$ active sites, thus further improving the catalytic activity of the catalyst [26, 97, 98]. (3) Free radical trapping agents, such as $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ , can be used to solve the compatibility problem [99, 100]. (4) The degree of graphitization of the catalyst can be improved, thus enhancing the antioxidation corrosion ability.[101, 102] (5)
Finally, other metal centers with high efficiency but weak Fenton reactions should be explored.
## 3  Classification of MOFs/PCPs as ORR Catalysts
## 3.1  Pristine MOFs/PCPs
For the vast majority of MOFs/PCPs, their conductivity is relatively poor because they are usually semiconductors or even insulators. Therefore, if they are not loaded with a conductive substrate, they are usually used as the precursor for carbonization so that they can be applied in electrocatalysis. However, it is possible to synthesize conductive MOFs/PCPs after adjusting the chemical composition and optimizing the reaction conditions [103]. Miner et al.[104] introduced $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (hexaiminotriphenylene) (Fig. 13a) as a conductive MOF/PCP, and this material exhibited strong catalytic properties for the ORR in alkaline aqueous solutions. Afterward, based on initial research, Miner et al.[105] found that after reacting with various types of metals, triphenylene ligands hexasubstituted by amino or phenol groups could provide a conductive, two-phase-layered 2D MOF. Their work provided solid evidence for other relevant studies. Sheberla et al.[106] used $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ as the electrode material for electrochemical double-layer capacitors, which was the first pure MOF/PCP applied in a supercapacitor without the addition of conductive substrates. To improve the conductivity of MOFs/PCPs, Yan et  al.[107] successfully induced unsaturated metal sites in a phytic acid${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ (Phy- ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ )-based MOF/PCP via room-temperature
## 3.2  MOF/PCP Composites
Although conductive MOFs/PCPs have already been synthesized, their electrocatalytic application is hindered because of their poor stability and conductivity under the electrocatalytic conditions of pure MOFs/PCPs [110]. Thus, the combination of MOFs/PCPs and other functional materials has been increasingly studied over the past few years. If we combine the following merits of MOFs/PCPs, such as their dispersed space structure and high porosity and conductivity, multifunctional materials for electrocatalysis can be achieved.
After combining a conductive carbon material and MOFs/ PCPs, the poor conductivity drawback of MOFs/PCPs can be solved, and the catalytic performance can be enhanced. The hydrothermal method is one of the methods to combine functional materials and MOFs/PCPs. Yin et al.[111] combined Co-OBA $\mathrm{\langleOBA=4,4^{\prime}}$ -oxybis (benzoic acid)) and carbon black via a hydrothermal method to synthesize a Co-OBA/C catalyst. In the Co-OBA/C catalyst, the ligand coordination mode of carboxylate is chelate coordination, and imidazole is the monodentate ligand. Compared with the Co-OBA and Co-OBA/C catalysts, the ORR, OER and bifunctional activities dramatically increase because of the interaction between Co-OBA and carbon black. Among the precursors for preparing carbon carriers, polyacrylonitrile (PAN) can provide conductivity after pyrolysis. The polymerization of nitrogen-rich materials with large specific surface areas at high temperatures favors the addition of active sites [112]. Qian et al.[113] combined salt-assisted pyrolysis and secondary polymerization of PAN at high temperature (Fig. 14) to synthesize an efficient and durable ORR electrocatalyst in an acidic medium. Zhong et al.[114] reported a copper phthalocyanine-based 2D conjugated MOF/PCP, which was obtained through the solvothermal method. Its stacked layer structure with $\mathrm{PcCu-O_{8}\mathrm{-}C o}$ dual ligands displayed excellent electrocatalytic ORR activity in an alkaline medium.
Metals and their composites can be combined with MOFs/PCPs and incorporated into the pores or substrates of MOFs/PCPs to prevent nanoparticles from aggregating during the synthesis process, ultimately enhancing the catalytic activity and stability of the final product [115]. Ling et al.[8] constructed a new artificial nanoenzyme on PMOF(Fe) formed by iron porphyrin and ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ ions (Fig. 15a–c). After loading very small $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ nanoparticles on the surface of PMOF(Fe), $\mathrm{Pt}@$ PMOF(Fe) was synthesized. Because of its large surface area and the exposed Fe active sites, this material could serve as a nanocarrier to prevent the agglomeration of Pt nanoparticles and display high peroxidase-like activity. Yin et al.[115] combined $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and MOF(Fe) to synthesize an $\varepsilon{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{MnO}_{2}/\mathbf{MOF}(\mathbf{Fe})$ composite. The $\mathbf{\varepsilon}_{\mathbf{\varepsilon}^{\mathrm{{c-MnO}}_{2}}}$ existed as nanorods with one end protruding out of the material and the other end anchored in the MOF(Fe) matrix. The unique structure of this composite facilitated the diffusion of oxygen and contact with $\scriptstyle\varepsilon-\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}^{+}$ during the reaction, which could improve the ORR activity and stability of $\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ under alkaline conditions. After that, this research group [116] used MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ to serve as the carrier to design and synthesize Co-based dual-function electrocatalysts (Fig. 15d–f and Table 1). During the synthesis process, they used $\mathrm{KBH}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ as a reductant and an oxidant, respectively, to etch the surface contents of $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ . The obtained electrocatalysts maintained an abundance of micropores and the main morphology of MIL-101(Cr). Additionally, all kinds of $\mathrm{Co}$ species were evenly dispersed on the surface of the electrocatalyst, which could to some degree increase the electrocatalytic activity of the material for the ORR and OER.
## 3.3  MOF/PCP Derivatives
Although some pure MOFs/PCPs and their composites display good electrocatalytic activity, the majority of them still cannot meet the requirements compared with MOF/ PCP derivatives due to the disadvantages of limited ion doping methods and poor catalytic activity. Most MOF/ PCP derivatives are carbon-based materials after the carbonization of the MOF/PCP. The internal organic ligand of MOFs/PCPs can transfer into a porous conductive carbon network, and the metal ions can be converted into metal nanoparticles in the carbon-based material, potentially providing active sites for catalyzing the ORR. It is worth noting that $Z\mathrm{n}$ is converted into $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (the boiling point of $2360^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) during carbonization, and the carbon generated from MOFs/PCPs in this process could reduce $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ back to Zn. Thus, higher porosity is obtained with the evaporation of $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms.[14]
In addition, doping can be performed during or after the carbonization process, which can provide more possibilities for the synthesis direction of MOF/PCP-derived electrocatalysts. There are many advantages of MOF/ PCP derivatives, such as their porous structures, high porosities, high conductivities, the convenient ion doping method and abundance of active sites [110].
## 3.3.1  Metal‑Loaded Carbon Material
MOFs/PCPs containing specific metals can be directly converted into metal nanoparticles after pyrolysis in an inert atmosphere, providing a sufficient number of electrocatalytic active sites. Moreover, after being loaded onto graphitic carbon, these particles improve the conductivity and stability of the entire nanohybrid material [20]. Guo et al.[117] selected cobalt-containing MOFs/PCPs with adenine as the ligand for pyrolysis, obtaining a cobalt nanoparticle-embedded, nitrogen-doped carbon/carbon nanotube framework $(\mathbf{Co}\textcircled{a}\mathbf{N}.$ CNTF). Because of the evenly dispersed active sites, the obtained Co@N-CNTF could serve as a trifunctional catalyst for further improving ORR, OER and HER activity. It is also possible to synthesize highly active single-atom catalysts by utilizing MOF/PCP derivatives. Deng et al.[118] used tannins to etch ZIF-8 precursors and generate a hollow structure (Fig. 16) that was treated with a Fe precursor containing iron acetylacetonate to achieve an atomic-level dispersion of Fe before being doped with $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ to generate an active structure with a high density of $\mathrm{Fe-N_{4}}$ . At the end of the above procedure, a hollow carbon polyhedron with a single-dispersed Fe atom and codoped N was fabricated, and this material showed excellent activity and stability for the
ORR in both acidic and alkaline aqueous solutions. MOF/ PCP derivatives modified with different methods can be used to synthesize MOFs/PCPs with varying morphology and composition. Agarwal et al.[119] synthesized an ORR catalyst templated with porous tellurium-doped ZIF-8 and studied the effect of replacing the $Z\mathrm{n}$ within ZIF-8 to Co. Notably, a loading of $10\%$ Co led to the best ORR and OER performance.
## 3.3.2  Metal‑Free Carbon Material
We can choose MOFs/PCPs with low boiling point metals $\scriptstyle(Z\ n-907\ ^{\circ}C$ , Cd- $767~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) as the precursor to synthesize metal-free carbon materials. During the carbonization process, the metal atoms evaporate at high temperature. The elimination of metal atoms increases the porosity of the catalyst. The other remaining metal atoms can be extracted by further acid leaching $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , [120] ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ , [121] HCl, [26], HF [122]).
Pyrolyzing MOFs/PCPs into carbon-based materials is the basic treatment for fabricating electrocatalysts. Additionally, pyrolyzing MOFs/PCPs with low boiling points into metal-free materials has provided inspiration for doping other metal heteroatoms and nonmetal heteroatoms. Zhao et al.[123] synthesized a porous carbon material that only contained carbon and oxygen by carbonizing $Z{\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ at $950^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The derivatives from this reaction displayed excellent electrocatalytic activity coupled with molecular selectivity and high stability. This material was a type of pure carbon without any other elemental doping and showed excellent ORR activity. After synthesizing the carbon material, it is also possible to proceed with doping various types of heteroatoms. Ye et al.[124] reported an efficient and handy method of pyrolyzing Zn-MOF-74 (Fig. 17) to obtain porous carbon and developed a reliable procedure to achieve tunable nitrogen doping. Their results showed that the nitrogen-doped porous carbon displayed excellent catalytic performance not only in alkaline media but also in acidic and neutral electrolytes.
## 4  Dimensionality of MOF/PCP‑Based Catalysts for the ORR
Various dimensional structures (0D, 1D, 2D, 3D) can be obtained after high-temperature pyrolysis and carbonization of MOFs/PCPs. The size effect of a catalyst is considered a key factor for the ORR. Therefore, the structure and size can be tuned by presynthesis design or postsynthesis modifications. The typical approach is to design and construct unique multidimensional, porous and hierarchical nanostructures with a high surface area to increase the exposure of active sites on the catalyst [125].
## 4.1  0D Nanostructure
0D materials contain polyhedrons, hollow and core–shell structures [21]. Among all ORR catalysts derived from MOFs/PCPs, polyhedrons are the most common structure. The catalytic activities of some typical MOF/PCP-derived 0D electrocatalysts for the ORR are summarized in Table 2. Wang et al.[126] tested the electrocatalytic performance of three common MOFs/PCPs (ZIF-8, ZIF-67, and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2})$ . It turned out that polyhedral ZIF-67 had the best electrocatalytic performance. The electrocatalytic activity of these MOFs/PCPs in acidic and alkaline solutions was increased after carbonization and acidolysis. Polyhedron materials are generally prepared through the external template method. Liu et al.[127] adopted a MOF as the precursor to furnish core–shell $@$ graphite carbon with polyhedral carbon doped with B and N, exhibiting a synergistic coupling effect among the B, C and N atoms. In addition, it can also be prepared by free-template pyrolysis using polystyrene, melamine, etc. Lee et al.[128] synthesized hollow HPC spheres with polystyrene $@$ ZIF-8 core–shell precursors after simple pyrolysis, which led to porous ZIF-8 transforming to porous carbon spheres without the initial structure collapsing (Fig. 18a). Tang et  al.[129] synthesized core–shell ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67 crystals (Fig.  18b–c) by a hydrothermal method. Then, with the process of carbonization and pyrolysis, the crystal demonstrated excellent electrocatalytic performance. Zhang et al.[130] constructed core–shell MO $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\alpha})\mathbf{F}@\mathbf{MOF}$ (Fig. 18d) with MOF [FJU-40-H (a) and FJU- $40\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (b)] in a nitrogen atmosphere. The obtained nitrogen-doped porous carbon material showed increased catalysis of the ORR. Moreover, the size of the hollow center and shell thickness could be controlled by the size and the concentration of the precursor, respectively [131].
## 4.2  1D Nanostructure
One-dimensional materials have a variety of structures, such as nanoropes, nanotubes and nanorods. Compared to 0D nanostructures, 1D nanomaterials have unique advantages in energy storage and device conversion. The catalytic activities of some typical 1D MOF/PCP-derived electrocatalysts for the ORR are summarized in Table 3. For example, 1D nanomaterials can provide faster mass and charge transfer routes while exposing more active sites. At the same time, 1D MOF/PCP-based derivatives display relatively high electrochemical performance [150–152].
The preparation methods for a 1D material can be categorized into self-template and external template methods [153]. The self-template method refers to the use of its own structure as a template for fabrication. Liu et al.[150] used Co–Ni-MOF-74 nanorods as a self-template to fabricate $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ mesoporous ternary nanorods by a microwave-assisted solvothermal method (Fig. 19a). The external template method always takes the external structure as the template to obtain the desired result. Generally, few MOFs/PCPs can use their own structure as a precursor template. The vast majority of 1D materials are prepared by the external template method. $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ is the most commonly used external template. It is stable at high temperature and can be removed with HF or an alkaline aqueous solution after pyrolysis. Liu et al.[154] used $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as the external template to obtain a $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ catalyst (Fig. 19b–d), which is very effective for CO oxidation. It is generally believed that the use of electrospinning technology to synthesize nanorods and nanotubes is the most commonly used method to synthesize one-dimensional materials. Liu et al.[151] used ZIF-8 as a precursor to synthesize 1D MOFs/PCPs (Fig. $19\mathrm{e-g}$ ) by electrospinning technology. Then, a high-temperature pyrolysis method was used to reasonably integrate $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles and Fe–N-doped mesoporous carbon nanofibers (called $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}@$ $\operatorname{FeN}@\operatorname{CNF})$ , and this material benefited from a high specific surface area and a good electron transfer rate. Furthermore, the abovementioned nanostructure displayed excellent electrocatalytic activity. Niu et  al.[46]. used electrospinning technology to codope $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}/\mathrm{{Co}}}$ -ZIF/PAN nanofibers with highly dispersed metals $\scriptstyle(Z\ n/C_{0})$ and heteroatoms (N) for preparing porous carbon nanofiber $(Z\boldsymbol n/$ $\mathbf{Co-N})@\mathbf{PCNF},$ membranes. This method not only produces a layered porous structure but also achieves a high distribution of metal active sites on the porous carbon nanofibers.
After the fabrication and carbonization of a 1D material, modification can still be achieved by doping other heteroatoms. Liu et al.[155] synthesized a pearl necklace fibrous carbon catalyst with electrospinning. After the carbonization and phosphatization of Zn/Fe-ZIF-PAN, Fe–N/Fe–P dual active sites (Fe–P/NHCF) were fabricated (Fig. 20a and b). Because of the doping-induced active sites, layered porosity and the 1D necklace network, the ORR activity and stability were greatly enhanced (Fig. 20c–e).
During the process of synthesizing 1D catalysts, the various calcination temperatures have a significant effect on catalytic performance. Yang et al.[156] fabricated PBA $@$ PAN nanofibers (Fig. 21a–d) by electrospinning. Additionally, they also studied the effects of different calcination temperatures on the catalytic ORR/OER activity of Co-FePBA and PAN. The highest dual-function electrocatalytic performance could be achieved at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 21e–g), with an $E_{1/2}$ of $0.869^{\scriptstyle\mathsf{V}}$ $\mathrm{\Delta}^{\prime}10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ ) and better stability and durability than $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ .
## 4.3  2D Nanostructure
The structure of 2D materials can be categorized as nanosheets coupled with nanoplates and nanofilms. 2D materials possess a larger surface area and more active sites, which play a significant role in mass and multiple metal ion transfer. The catalytic activities of some typical MOF/PCPderived 2D electrocatalysts for the ORR are summarized in Table 4. Compared to the materials with other dimensions, 2D structures have myriad applications in the field of energy storage and conversion. In addition, ultrathin 2D nanomaterials are of great interest due to their excellent confinement of electrons [18], which is facilitated due to the generation of multiple cavities or channels in the structure [51].
The preparation method for 2D MOF/PCP thin films can be divided into the in situ growth method and the crystal secondary growth method. The in situ growth method refers to the method in which the crystal is not attached to the surface of the carrier in advance, and the layered material is obtained by growing by itself in the original solution. Only a few MOF/PCP film materials can be successfully synthesized due to the weak interface keying of $\mathrm{MOFs}/\$ PCPs to germinate by themselves. Thus, the crystal secondary growth method is more common. The commonly used templates are as follows: $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , graphite, the metal mesh, $Z\mathrm{nO}$ , and polyamide. The synthesis methods can be sorted into direct growth, layer-by-layer growth, secondary growth, chemical solution precipitation, electrostatic spinning, microwave and other methods [163]. In 2005, Fischer et al.[164] fabricated the first coordination thin film with the modification of a functional supporter during the process of MOF-5 deposition. In 2010, Centrone et al.[165] successfully obtained MOF/PCP thin films on polyacrylonitrile substrates by the microwave method. Apart from polyacrylonitrile, $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ can also be used as a template for a 2D substrate while also being a source of nitrogen. Zheng et al. used a dual-metal MOF/PCP $(Z{\mathrm{n}}/{\mathrm{Fe}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MOF}})$ as a precursor and $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ as a nitrogen source to synthesize porous carbon nanosheets (Fig. 22a) with many single/dual Fe $(3.89\mathrm{wt}\%)$ and N $(10.28~\mathrm{wt}\%)$ atoms. Researchers have found that the addition of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ plays a key role in achieving the high loading of single/dual Fe atoms, and the 2D nanosheet structure provides the material with a large surface area and high porosity, thereby displaying better ORR catalytic activity in both circumstances [166]. In addition, creating defects in 2D structures can also enhance catalytic ORR activity in acidic electrolytes. Tan et al.[167] used a 2D ( $\mathrm{Zn}$ -ZIF-L) material rather than a 3D material (Zn-ZIF-8) as the precursor, which was unraveled into a nitrogen-doped graphene nanomesh by using LiCl and KCl as exfoliators and etching agents. Due to the 2D structure, the nitrogen-doped graphene nanomesh exhibits excellent catalytic activity for the ORR in acid electrolytes, which is important for metal-free carbon-based electrocatalysis in acidic systems.
In addition, 2D MOFs/PCPs can be categorized into two different types. One is top-down, and the other is bottomup, including sonication exfoliation, three-layer synthesis and interfacial synthesis [168]. The top-down type needs to exfoliate the layered bulk MOFs/PCPs, while the bottom-up type needs to directly synthesize 2D MOF/PCP nanosheets with the combination of metal and organic precursors, which means that desirable 2D MOFs/PCPs can be synthesized directly from the center metal [169]. The interaction between MOF/PCP layers is weak; however, because there are strong planar coordination bonds, 2D MOFs/PCPs are usually obtained from block layered MOFs/PCPs based on the topdown peeling method. Using a suitable interfacial medium and the surfactant can constrain the growth of MOFs/PCPs in the vertical direction to the layer during the bottom-up synthesis process so that the lateral growth forms a 2D layered structure [168]. Recently, Lv et al.[38] adopted a bottom-up strategy (Fig. 22b–e) to fabricate a sandwich-like precious metal-free electrocatalyst with highly dispersed $\mathrm{Fe-N}_{x}$ active sites embedded in a heterogeneous layered porous carbon/ graphene. This catalyst had, a half-wave potential that reached $0.88\mathrm{V}_{;}$ , along with excellent ORR activity, high durability and good methanol resistance. Chai et al.[134] adopted the same strategy to synthesize porous carbon ball derivatives modified with MOFs/PCPs (Fig. 22f).
## 4.4  3D Nanostructure
3D ORR catalysts are formed by connecting blocks in a variety of shapes, such as honeycombs, flowers, sponges, and meshes [21]. A three-dimensional structure has the merits of reducing the mass diffusion length, multiple interfaces and low density. The open structure is conducive to exposing a large number of active sites, which can also dramatically accelerate the reduction and emission of oxygen [143, 176]. Liu et al.[74] successfully fabricated 3D porous $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Co-NPC}$ by doping P into flower-like $\mathrm{Co}/$ Zn-MOF (Fig. 23a) during the carbonization process, and this material displayed high catalytic activity; for the ORR reaction, its half-wave potential can reach $0.872{\mathrm{~V~}}$ . The catalytic activities of some typical MOF/PCP-derived 3D electrocatalysts for the ORR are summarized in Table 5.
Notably, 3D materials can be synthesized in a single step or a 2D material can be used as a precursor for further synthesis. Kim et al.[177] obtained hexagonal pillar-shaped Zn-MOF-74 (Fig. 23b–f) via the solvothermal method, with diameters ranging from 300 to $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ . After successfully carbonizing Zn-MOF-74@CNF for the first time, researchers obtained a 3D structure among C-Zn-MOF-74, CNFs and C-Zn-MOF-C, which facilitated electron transfer. Recently, a research group at the Chinese Academy of Sciences used ZIF-I to design an egg yolk-shell-structured ZIF-67@HMCS hybrid material. The core and shell of this structure were ZIF-67 and hollow mesoporous carbon spheres (HMCS), respectively. With the utilization of the space confinement effect of HMCS to control the size of ZIF-67, the diffusion route was shortened, thereby increasing the rate of ion transfer. Thus, the conductivity of ZIF-67 can be significantly enhanced by sealing ZIF-67 into HMCS [178]. Moreover, the typical layered porous structure of HMCS ensures that the reactant quickly and effectively disperses to the active sites exposed by ZIF-67, which increases electrochemical activity. In addition, the 3D structure is conducive to forming a high graphitization degree and a hollow carbon structure [84]. The large surface area of the 3D structure and the mutually connected pores within it can largely increase the number of available triphase active sites and facilitate the transfer between $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and the electrolyte. Wu et al.[179] used $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ as a template (Fig. $23\mathrm{g}$ ) and $\mathrm{C}_{24}\mathrm{H}_{20}\mathrm{BrP}$ as a phosphorus source to synthesize phosphorus-doped hierarchical porous carbon (P-HPC) at high temperature. The results showed that P-HPC had excellent ORR activity and endurance because of the 3D porous structure.
## 5  Active Sites of MOF/PCP‑Based Catalysts
## 5.1  Active Site of a Metal‑Free Atom
Among all the carbon materials derived from MOFs/PCPs, the doping of heteroatoms into MOFs/PCPs (such as N, S, P, Se, and O) can change the porous structure and adjust the electronic structure, thus favoring mass and electron transfer and the generation of active species [193]. Heteroatom doping is a fundamental mechanism by which carbon materials promote the four-electron ORR pathway [123]. Among them, phosphides (such as CoP, $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{P}_{3}$ , ${\mathrm{Ni}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ , MoP, WP and FeMnP), chalcogenides (such as $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{WS}_{2}$ , amorphous $\mathbf{MoS}_{x}$ , $\mathrm{WSe}_{2}\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ and NiSe) and phosphorous sulfides (such as MoPS, CoPS and FePS) have been increasingly studied because of their notable activity and stability [194].
## 5.1.1  N Active Site
To improve the catalytic activity of MOF/PCP-based electrocatalysts, MOFs/PCPs are usually carbonized or composited with carbon-based materials (such as graphene, carbon nanotubes and nanocarbon materials) to enhance the electrical conductivity. Heteroatoms are then doped into carbon nanomaterials, which can effectively tune the electronic performance and surface structure so that the electrochemical properties are remarkably elevated [195]. Among MOF/ PCP-derived carbon materials or doped carbon materials, the main active site falls on the defective site of carbon.
The methods of generating carbon defects include ball milling, high-temperature treatment and ion etching. Raman characterization is commonly carried out to determine the carbon defect degree. The D-band is related to the disorganized structure on the surface of graphite and defects in the carbon material. However, the G-band features the sp2-hybridized bond of graphite carbon, which indicates the degree of graphitization. The defect or graphitization degree of carbon-containing materials is always measured with the $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio. A higher $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ indicates the presence of more defective sites. To compare the activity between flat carbon and edge carbon, Li et al.[196] first developed undoped edge-rich graphene as a truly efficient nonmetal electrocatalyst by controlling the temperature and processing time (Fig. 24); this technique considerably increased the number of edge sites on the surface of graphene. As observed in Fig. 24f, the ratio of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ increased from 1.16 to 1.51 after plasma treatment, indicating that the number of defects increased on the graphene surface. This result indicated that the number of defective sites could be largely increased due to the number of pores and edge sites generated from plasma treatment.
The above researcher also found that the edge of carbon was more active than the base surface and higher than inplane sp2-hybridized carbon atoms. Their report pointed out that defective atoms had a higher charge density, which provided absorption sites for the ORR reaction. Researchers have used MOFs/PCPs with different pore size distributions as precursors and some carbon materials (graphene, carbon nanotubes, carbon nanoparticles) as carriers and then adopted high-temperature heat treatment to prepare composite materials of MOFs/PCPs and carbon materials. This technique takes advantage of the large specific surface area of the MOFs/PCPs and the good electrical conductivity of the carbon materials, substantially increasing ORR performance. Furthermore, encapsulating metal nanoparticles into a carbon structure can provide a special synergistic effect and long-term performance, which can activate the atoms around nanoparticles and the other surface atoms with corrosion endurance [174]. In addition, when MOFs/PCPs with transition metal centers are carbonized, the observed transition metal carbides show an effective ORR performance due to their d-band electronic density of states being similar to that of $\mathrm{Pt}.$ -group metals [197].
Nitrogen-doped carbon materials are one of the most common materials for ion doping. The three common nitrogen sites (Fig. 25a) with oxygen reduction activity are pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, and graphitic N. Pyrrolic N and graphitic N are in charge of decreasing the work function, while pyridinic N is conducive to increasing the density of states as a potential active site [81]. The best site for catalytic ORR performance has not yet been determined. In 2014, Liang et al.[198] studied the correlation between
ORR activity and the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ activation temperature (Fig. 25b), pointing out that graphitic $\mathbf{N}$ was likely to have a positive effect on ORR activity in an alkaline medium. In 2017, Guo et al.[199] designed a highly oriented pyrolyzed graphite catalyst (Fig. 25c–f) to characterize the ORR active sites, which revealed clear p-conjugation and well-controlled N species doping. The ORR active sites were produced by pyridine diazide. The carbon dioxide absorption experiment indicated that pyridine diazide could generate Lewis base sites. The specific activity of each pyridinic N in the highly oriented pyrolytic graphite model catalyst was equivalent to that of a N-doped graphene powder catalyst. Thus, the ORR active sites of a N-doped carbon material are the carbon atoms with pyridinic N as a Lewis base.
Moreover, there is a nearly linear correlation between the catalytic ORR activity and pyridinic N content (Fig. 26a and b) [140]. It is generally believed that pyridinic N can reduce the energy barrier of the adjacent carbon atom, which accelerates the first electron transfer that typically demonstrates sluggish kinetics during the ORR procedure. However, graphitic $\mathbf{N}$ can increase $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ absorption and subsequently detach/weaken the O–O bond by inducing uneven electron distribution and short $\mathrm{C-N}$ bonds (equivalent to O–O bonds) [145]. Regarding carbon-based catalysts doped with nitrogen, Yu et al.[200] fabricated carbon nanotubes (CNTs)
doped with B and N (Fig. 26c–e). The electrochemical test demonstrated that the ORR performance in the bonding case gradually decreased to the level of the inert original carbon nanotubes with an increasing B/N ratio. As the B and N contents increased, the onset potential and current density during separation improved. This phenomenon shows that nitrogen doping has broken the initial sp2-hybridized electronic structure balance of the carbon atoms, which increases the density of active sites and improves ORR performance. The crucial part of heteroatom doping is the activation of an electron, which can be used by oxygen molecules, rather than whether the heteroatom itself is electron-rich or electron-deficient [201].
N-doping synthesis strategies can be categorized into two types: (1) direct carbonization of nitrogen-containing MOF/ PCP (ZIF-8, ZIF-67) precursors, which is called “in situ” nitrogen doping, and (2) postsynthesis of a carbonization process modification strategy (such as ammonia, acetonitrile, and dicyandiamide [202]. Zhou et al.[87] synthesized brand-new 3D star-like hybrid MOF/PCP (Fig. 27a–c), carbonizing ZIF-8 and ZIF-L at $910^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an Ar atmosphere. Zn ions evaporated into zinc vapor to form a porous structure and doped N atoms into the material. Hu et al.[170] blended MIL-100(Fe) with polyaniline powder through grinding and heated it in $10\%$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ $90\%$ argon) to synthesize a $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{NC}$ nitrogen-containing catalyst (Fig. 27d–f), demonstrating a modification strategy with polyaniline and ammonia gas.
Different pyrolysis temperatures can generate various contents of graphitic nitrogen, pyridinic nitrogen and pyrrolic nitrogen. Ma et al.[160] selected a new dicyandiamidebased Co-MOF (a and b) as the single precursor to fabricate N-doped carbon nanotubes, which not only served as the nitrogen source but also facilitated the generation of NCNTs (nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes) because of its induction effect during the calcination of the graphite structure. The XPS survey showed that the content of pyrrolic N was the highest at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 28c), which was conducive to the positive onset potential for the ORR. Moreover, an appropriate amount of graphitic N can facilitate the adsorption of oxygen atoms by absorbing electrons from neighboring atoms. The results showed that the ORR had the highest onset potential and the half-wave potential at a pyrolysis temperature of $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 28d).
Roy et al.[25] introduced a strategy that fabricated a primitive electrochemically active conjugated microporous polymer (CMP) (Fig. 29) by connecting the donor node and the acceptor spacer and calculating the oxygen molecule binding pattern and energy of two donor–acceptor systems. The oxygen molecule could be bonded to the nitrogen site in a head-on way because the nitrogen was in the chargeseparated electropositive state. Likewise, since the structure of the CMP was comprised of a suitable donor site and an acceptor spacer, these two systems had relatively high conductivity, which led to easy electron transfer throughout the entire system. The dense arrangement of nitrogen sites made the combination of oxygen molecules easier and electrocatalytic reduction possible.
One of the common methods of modifying nitrogen doping after carbonization is to inject ammonia gas as an etching treatment. A higher density of the pyridinic N dopant and a higher porosity can be integrated into the N-doped metalfree carbon-based ORR catalyst. The precarbonization of N-containing MOFs/PCPs at high temperature results in a high degree of graphitization, and the subsequent $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ etching makes the fabricated carbon have a higher content of pyridinic N sites and higher porosity without changing the high degree of carbonization [140]. The $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ treatment plays an essential role in adjusting the relative content between pyridinic N and graphitic N. This procedure can have an impact on N species and optimize the porosity and nitrogen structure of NPC, leading to a large specific surface area, layered porous structure and a high content of graphitic nitrogen [145]. Song et al.[146] found that the proportion of pyridinic N increased after the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ treatment of ZIF8, while the proportion of graphitic $\mathbf{N}$ decreased (Fig. 30).
According to the XPS survey results, the heating process of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is conducive to transforming graphitic N into pyridinic N [190]. The methods for doping nitrogen into MOF/PCPderived catalysts include chemical vapor deposition, mixed carbonization solid-phase synthesis and solvothermal liquid phase synthesis. In contrast, the nitrogen doping content of solid-phase synthesis is much larger [139]. Polyaniline (PANI) is a type of conductive nitrogen-containing polymer. It is usually doped with nitrogen by in situ polymerization, fixing a thin PANI layer of uniform thickness on the surface of MOFs/PCPs to obtain oligomeric aniline (OANI)-coated MOFs/PCPs. Then, a nitrogen-containing carbon material can be synthesized via pyrolysis and carbonization. There are several merits of the in situ polymerization of PANI for nitrogen doping, such as its easy operation, few limitations for the MOF/PCP structure, increased conductivity to provide a nitrogen source and decreased metal agglomeration.
## 5.1.2  S Active Site
S species act as a “sacrificial carrier” to increase the diffusion of Fe species via Fe-S coordination and generate a porous alveolar structure by escaping in the form of $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ during pyrolysis [86]. Sun et al. first indicated that the N,Scoupled dopant could separate many more active sites than N and S alone, indicating that the $-S C\mathrm{-C-N-}$ bond structure where N and S come close to each other and dope in the same carbon atom has the possibility to become a doping site with higher ORR activity. N and S codoped nanocarbons can be synthesized by adjusting the porous structure derived from MOFs/PCPs to serve as the ORR catalyst. This research group has also used ZIF-8 as a precursor to design carbon holes and apertures, obtaining more active sites after codoping with sulfur. The first-principles calculation shows that the main reason for the notable electrochemical performance is the synergistic effect between the $\mathbf{N}$ and S dopants [146]. El-Sawy et al.[203] developed a new bistable strategy (Fig. 31a and b). Briefly, carbon nanotubes were oxidized by the Hummer method, then the $\mathbf{O}_{x}$ -CNTs were heated with thiourea via hydrothermal treatment; finally, the samples were heated in $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ to control the active sites of sulfur atom-doped carbon nanotubes. This group was the first to demonstrate how to adjust active sites by doping precursors so that the electrochemical activity was well controlled (Fig. 31c–e). Metal phosphides and heteroatom-doped carbon are regarded as catalysts suitable for the ORR.
$\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ is a representative sulfide photocatalyst but has been scarcely studied in the field of electrocatalysis. 1-T phase $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ has a large number of exposed planar active sites and the advantage of low cost [204, 205]. Amiinu et al.[191] reported a notable electrocatalyst with a Mo–N-C framework and a Mo–N interfacial coupling center encapsulated (Fig. 32) by vertically arranged $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets. Its heterostructure exhibited excellent catalytic activity and stability for the ORR, HER and OER.
## 5.1.3  P Active Site
It is worth noting that P doping can generate charge delocalization and asymmetric spin density of carbon atoms, which improve ORR kinetics [189]. Additionally, the synergistic effect between P–C bonds further enhances the catalytic performance of carbon toward the ORR [206].
It is possible that the active sites will disappear with the presence of anions in an acidic aqueous solution [76]. Najam et al.[185] meticulously controlled the growth of ZIF-8 on Fe-PBA to obtain cubes and established P and N codoped Fe-based carbon after pyrolysis. This material could be used in alkaline/acidic media without very large losses of active sites due to its improved structural stability. P doping could elevate the catalytic performance of a Fe–NC catalyst, with $S\mathrm{O}_{x}.$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ and $\mathrm{PO}_{x}$ toxicity resistance. For comparison, N-doped carbon (NC) and $_\mathrm{Fe,N}$ codoped carbon (Fe–NC) were fabricated by direct pyrolysis with ZIF-8 and Fe-PBA, respectively. After the second $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ doping, Fe-PNC and PNC were obtained, leading to improved catalytic performance after P doping. Ahn et al.[207] used the in situ crosslinking method (Fig. 33a) to impregnate a high content of cobalt phosphide into a synthesized carbon material, obtaining a series of 1D hybrid catalysts composed of ultrathin cobalt phosphide nanoparticles supported by a carbon substrate. This method also maintained the small nanoparticle size of the cobalt phosphide. The obtained catalysts displayed notable dual-function activity and durability. The EDS results (Fig. 33b) demonstrated that the P doping was successful. With a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , the half-wave potential of the ORR and the potential of the OER decreased to $0.90\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}}$ and $1.64\mathrm{~V}_{\mathrm{RHE}}$ , respectively. (Fig. 33c–e) The ultrahigh dual-function activity could be ascribed to the synergistic effect observed among the doped cobalt phosphide nanoparticles, N, S and P.
## 5.1.4  Se Active Site
As one of the chalcogen elements, Se demonstrates relatively similar chemical properties to S. For example, the electronegativity of Se is quite close to S, with one at 2.55 and the other at 2.58. In addition, the atomic size of Se is larger than that of S. Since doping S into graphene can improve the electrocatalytic performance of graphene, researchers have begun to use a carbon material doped with Se as a metal-free catalyst for the ORR [208].
Controlling the active sites for a nonmetal catalyst is a crucial part of enhancing its catalytic performance. Palanivelu et al.[209] managed to control the nitrogen content of a catalyst with the addition of Se. An M–N-C catalyst was synthesized via a high-pressure hydrothermal method. Compared to iron, an M–N-C-3 catalyst with a nominal selenium content of $14\mathrm{at}\%$ was determined to be the best ORR catalyst among the synthesized catalysts. Park et al.[210] used ZIF-8 as the precursor to synthesize CNT/graphene composites doped with Se via a simple annealing procedure (Fig. 34a). This unique structure could facilitate penetration from chain selenium to micropores and make it easier for the electrolyte to enter through the mesopores. The Se-doped catalysts exhibited excellent cycling and rate performance due to the low-range ordering of chain selenium and good conductivity of the carbon material. The author suggested that the good electrocatalytic performance originated from the chain selenium and ring selenium inside the catalyst (Fig. 34b–f).
## 5.2  Transition Metal Active Sites
Transitional metal doping (such as Fe, Co, Mn, Cu and Ni) was found to strengthen the electron-occupied states and introduce hybridized orbitals near the Fermi level, enhancing the charge transformation and ORR performance [194]. Recent research has shown that the ORR activity of transition metals follows the order $\mathrm{Fe}>\mathrm{Co}>\mathrm{Cu}>\mathrm{Mn}>\mathrm{Ni}$ . Furthermore, the synergistic effect between different metal phases can significantly improve ORR and OER activity [211]. Researchers often use MOF/PCP-based materials as precursors to obtain M–N-C-like structures via pyrolysis or pickling due to the high ORR activity of M–N-C-like catalysts. The active sites of M–N-C-like catalysts have always been a hot topic for research.
The M–N-C material, derived from MOFs/PCPs, has demonstrated a wide range of application prospects because of its unique structural properties, particularly catalysts based on the M–N-C structure [74]. Moreover, M–N-C electrocatalysts have been increasingly studied due to their low cost, notable catalytic activity and chemical stability [212]. The periodically organized active sites in MOFs/ PCPs, including $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{x},\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathbf{S}}_{x}$ and $\mathbf{M}{-}\mathbf{O}_{x}$ units (M is commonly Fe, Co, Ni, etc.), are the most applicable electrocatalytic active centers [42]. In addition, a combination of metal ions is conducive to improving the electrochemical activity of single metal MOF/PCP nanosheets. In addition to the metal ion activity, the coordinated unsaturated metal sites can also enhance the ORR activity of MOFs/PCPs [17]. Regarding nitrogen-doped carbon materials, metal ions and nitrogen have the capability to boost electrocatalytic activity at low concentrations [194]. Transition metal alloys (such as NiMo and CoMo), phosphides (such as $\mathbf{CoP}$ , $\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{P};$ , ${\mathrm{Ni}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}_{3}$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ , MoP, WP and FeMnP), chalcogenides (e.g., $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{WS}_{2}$ , amorphous $\mathbf{MoS}_{x}$ , $\mathsf{W S e}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ , and NiSe) and phosphosulfides (e.g., MoPS, CoPS, and FePS) have been increasingly studied due to their excellent activity and stability [43].
## 5.2.1  Fe Active Site
Among M–N-C catalysts, Fe–N-C is a type of catalyst with the highest catalytic performance. Thus, the vast majority of studies have focused on Fe–N-C catalysts. There are two types of Fe in the catalysts. One is atomically dispersed amorphous iron, and the other is crystalline iron or $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ in the form of nanoparticles. Although the doping of Fe atoms can dramatically improve catalytic ORR performance, there is still controversy about the specific active site of Fe.
Wang et  al.[184] replaced $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ with MIL-101(Fe) (Fig. 36) so that the agglomeration of Fe-based particles could be avoided. In addition, selecting ZIF-8 as the protective layer could maintain its structure during the pyrolysis process and obtain $\mathrm{Fe-N}_{x}$ edge sites. Carbon nanotubes could be synthesized with the addition of Fe ions and hydrazine hydrate during MOF/PCP precursor synthesis. Since hydrazine hydrate serves as an alkaline reducing agent, it not only erodes ZIF-8 but also prevents $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ from oxidizing to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , which harms ZIF-8 due to its acidity. It is worth noting that the direct addition of Fe salt and hydrazine hydrate into the MOF/PCP precursor tended to destroy the 3D frame structure [186].
## 5.2.2  Co Active Site
Since the $\mathrm{Fe-N_{4}}$ active site has been found, $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ has also been discovered to possess excellent catalytic performance. Moreover, Co has better stability than Fe. Although Co particles are likely to agglomerate, Co coated with carbon will not be easily eliminated. Notably, 3D MOFs/PCPs have provided the possibility to construct high-density catalysts. Ma et al.[48] found that imidazolate groups could transform into carbonaceous forms during the thermal activation process (Fig. 37 a). ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ could coordinate with pyridinic or pyrrolic nitrogen atoms to synthesize stable ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ which epitomized the principle of $\mathrm{Co-N_{4}}$ generation.
Co nanoparticles play a positive role in the ORR, facilitating the graphitization degree of carbon [220]. According to a report, the unique (112) plane (Fig. 37b–e) surrounding the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -based nanocomposites has outstanding electrochemical activity for catalyzing the ORR compared to its counterparts (Fig. 37f and g). The surface atomic arrangement of $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ active sites, particularly the existence of octahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ sites, optimizes the adsorption, activation and desorption characteristics of oxygen species [221]. DFT calculations show that the cobalt atom is coordinated with $3\mathrm{N}$ atoms and 1 C atom, which is conducive to ORR activity and accelerates the ORR compared to the traditional cobalt atom doped with $4\mathrm{N}$ atoms [87]. In addition, electrocatalytic activity also comes from single-atom (SA) metal sites. Guo et al.[222] showed a coupled evolution of both cobalt metal sites and $\operatorname{Co}@\operatorname{C}@\operatorname{N}$ sites in the pyrolysis progress of $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}/\mathrm{Co}$ bimetallic MOFs/PCPs, which was confirmed by advanced X-ray spectroscopy and electron microscopy characterizations. The results indicated that single-atom cobalt sites induced the four-electron catalytic pathway of the ORR.
It is crucial to develop an electrocatalyst with various types of active sites and optimal structures. Wang et al.[83] reported a melting salt-assisted solvent-free method (Fig. 38a–e) for the in situ incorporation of Fe or Co into MOF/PCP nanocrystals to construct Zn (M) IDA MOFs $\mathbf{\mathrm{{M}=F e}}$ and Co). Then, an M–N-C material is obtained after carbonization. These materials have four merits: (1) high porosity, (2) high content of nitrogen heteroatoms, (3) highly distributed Fe or Co nanoparticles and (4) transition metal nanoparticles shielded by graphitic carbon shells to increase conductivity and prevent leaching due to highly corrosive electrolytes. Apart from the positive ORR effect provided by ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ , SA–Co is also an important contributor to catalytic activity. To prove the ORR activity of SA-Co, Zhao et al.[187] used sulfur-assisted pyrolysis purification (Fig. 38f) to remove metal particle sites. The catalyst after the complete removal of metal sites displayed no decrease in its ORR and HER activity. Moreover, as the ORR selectivity increased, the single-atom electrocatalysis of the SA-Co site was considered to play a significant role in the ORR and HER process among the MOF/PCP derivatives. The space structure and pyrolysis temperature could partially influence the Co-based catalyst. Diaz-Duran et al.[149] used three types of Co-MOFs combined with linear-coordinated MOFs/PCPs and a coordinator to obtain different dimensions of nitrogen-doped mesoporous carbon at various pyrolysis temperatures (Table 6). This group drew several conclusions, which are as follows: (1) pyrolysis at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ produced the lowest content of Co and $\mathbf{N}$ and the highest content of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , proving that ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ and pyridinic N could decrease the amount of ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ ; (2) the porous structure was beneficial for maintaining the $\mathbf{N}$ content; (3) the higher the
N content was, the more active the catalyst was; and (4) the hollow structure could provide an active site channel.
## 5.2.3  Cu Active Site
Cu has been widely used as a catalyst in the field of energy conversion [223]. Even if its electrocatalytic activity is worse than Fe-, Co- and Ni-based TMP catalysts, Cu has shown excellent catalytic ORR performance with proximity to Pt in the “volcano plot” [224]. Elevating the electrocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ requires effectively adjusting the coordination environment of the Cu center.[147] Wang et al.[189] were the first to use Cu-based MOFs/PCPs as a single precursor and template (Fig. 39 a) to fabricate $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{P}@$ NPPC electrocatalysts doped with N and P, which displayed excellent ORR activity. The connecting ligand with different structures and nitrogen contents subtly had an impact on the type of cobalt oxide. Wu et al.[225] also grew various MOFs/PCPs on carbon cloth (Fig. $39\mathrm{d-g}{\cdot}$ ), functionalizing the carbon cloth with trace amounts of cobalt oxide after pyrolysis. These disparate composites had different Co-active sites, owing to their excellent stability and limited current density. Different $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ loadings could lead to a change in the coordination environment, with the presence of different heteroactive sites. Lai et al.[147] used ZIF-8 as a precursor and doped it with $\mathtt{C u}$ atoms (Fig. 39h–i). After the carbonization process, the $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ structure was synthesized. The states of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ could be effectively adjusted by changing the loading amount of Cu. Thus, highly active heterocoordination sites of N, $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ were finally constructed. The optimized $25\%$ $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ catalyst displayed excellent ORR activity and stability in a $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH aqueous solution.
## 5.2.4  Noble Metal‑Free Active Site
## 6  Conclusion and Prospects
In general, MOF/PCP-based ORR catalysts have been increasingly studied. In this review, we discussed the ORR mechanism, performance, design principles of ORR catalysts, different precursors of MOF/PCP-based ORR catalysts (ZIF-8, ZIF-67, MIL-101, MOF-74), synthesis methods, classification of precursors (pure MOFs/PCPs, MOF/ PCP composites, MOF/PCP derivatives), stability of MOF/
PCP-based ORR catalysts in acidic media (reasons, solutions), active sites of metal-free atoms (N, S, P, Se, etc.) and transition metal active sites (Fe, Co, Cu, etc.), and different dimensions of MOF/PCP-based ORR catalysts (0D, 1D, 2D, 3D). Additionally, we summarized the latest progress in the past five years and the prospects for the future direction of MOF/PCP-based ORR catalysts. To explore a highly efficient ORR catalyst, it is necessary to select MOFs/PCPs with suitable metal ions and organic ligands, pyrolyze at a high temperature and dope heteroatoms during pyrolysis.
To date, the electrocatalytic performance of MOF/PCPbased ORR catalysts has surpassed that of precious metal catalysts in alkaline media. However, it is still not possible to entirely replace precious metal catalysts. (1) The stability of the ORR catalyst in acidic media needs to be further improved. The key point to enhance the stability of catalysts in acidic media is to reduce the production of reactive oxygen species. The cooperative effects of different metal atom sites and metal-free sites can be used to adjust the electronic structure of the active site. Moreover, we can decrease the dissolution of metal particles and decrease the oxidation of carbon by improving the graphitization degree of carbon and preparing single-atom catalysts. (2) The structural collapse of MOFs/PCPs during pyrolysis still remains. We can focus on the pretreatment of precursors while also using different synthesis methods to change the microstructure of catalysts. Several methods have been proposed for preventing structural collapse, such as precursor design and aqueous solution pretreatment to stabilize MOFs/PCPs. (3) It is still a challenge to accurately control the amount, dispersion and generation of active sites. More detailed characterization should be carried out to investigate the active sites and further explain the catalytic mechanism process. We can consider both the catalytic performance and preparation conditions together through systematic research work and provide guidance for the theoretical design and preparation of new catalysts. (4) The preparation conditions of hightemperature pyrolysis are less novel. Catalysts with good performance are mostly composed of single-metal active or double-metal active sites with coordinated N. However, the metal site concentration is not high, and the nonnoble metal active site density is low due to high-temperature pyrolysis. We can regulate the intrinsic activity of the active site from the aspect of its electronic structure. In addition, the cocatalyst of nonprecious metals and trace precious metals might be the choice for future practical applications.
These findings contribute in several ways to our understanding of MOFs/PCPs as promising precursors for the fabrication of ORR catalysts in energy storage and catalysis. Derivatives of MOFs/PCPs have shown excellent catalytic ORR properties. Although there are still some problems in the application for the ORR, it is possible to develop better ORR catalysts with excellent stability and outstanding performance. We believe that the abovementioned difficulties and challenges will be eliminated in the future. The findings of this review provide a reference for researchers in the field of MOFs/PCPs for electrocatalysis.
